# Both images build from the repo ROOT (they need the root package-lock.json —
# see server/Dockerfile for why), so the build context is the whole repo. Keep
# it small, and above all keep host node_modules out: they are Windows/arm64
# builds and would overwrite what npm ci installs for linux/amd64.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
dist/
**/dist/
build/

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.truenas.example

.git/
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

coverage/
playwright-report/
test-results/
*.log

# Not needed to build or run the app
docs/
design_handoff_new_tab/
newt-brand/
scripts/

# ...with two exceptions, both of which run at the end of the client build and
# fail it on a defect that is invisible everywhere else.
#
# check-html.mjs fails the build if the built index.html doesn't actually load
# the application - a thing that has shipped (see the note in client/index.html).
# check-css.mjs fails it if a frosted surface came out of the minifier naming
# only the -webkit- alias of backdrop-filter, which no browser implements - also
# a thing that has shipped, across every panel in the app.
#
# Neither guard would have caught its own bug from a developer's machine alone,
# because both bugs are in what the *production* build emits. So they have to be
# in the image build, so they have to be in the context. Docker applies the last
# matching pattern, so these re-include the two files without the rest.
#
# Anything added here needs the matching COPY in client/Dockerfile: the build
# names each script by hand rather than copying the directory.
!scripts/check-html.mjs
!scripts/check-css.mjs
